*******************************************************************************
Partition table
*******************************************************************************
Overview
	- data in boot sector of the disk(sector 0)
	- tells how the disk is divided into drives
	- tells how data is organised on drive
		- it knows the bootloader, OS
	- types
		-aix
		- amiga
		- bsd
		- dvh
		- gpt
		- mac
		- msdos --> mbr
		- pc98
		- sun
		- loop	--> raw disk access without partition

Popularly used	 types
	- formated as FAT/NTFS	
	- MBR[Master Boot Record]
		- BIOS	[Basic Input Output System]
		- Sector 0 [First sector]
			- 512 bytes
				- [446 bytes] 	Master Boot Code
							- bootloader
				- [64 bytes]	partition table info
							- primary and extended
				- [2 bytes ]	MBR signature
							- 0x55AA --> indicates valid MBR	
		- total 64bytes	-> 4 entries
			- only 4 primary partitions can be created
		- out of 4, one can be created as extended partition 
			- extended partition can further be divided into 23 partitions 
		- supports partition upto 2TB
		- data recover is difficult
		
	
	- GPT[GUID Partition table]
		- globally unique identifier[GUID]
		- Sector 1 	
				- Sector 0 kept empty for MBR,for backward compatibility
		- UEFI	[Unified Extensible Firmware Interface]
		- 128 entries
		- supports partition upto 9.7 Billion TB
		- data recovery is possible as backup partiton is created
		
Linux utilities:
CLI:
	- fdisk		: MBR
	- gdisk 	: GPT
	- parted	: MBR & GPT
GUI:	
	- gparted	: MBR & GPT
	
Analysis
1. ddi image os mounted as a loop device
	losetup -f <ddi image>
	
2. Check for ddi image loop device
	losetup -l
	/dev/loop23         0      0         0  0 <path_to_image>.ddi   0     512

Commands to check for type of partition
3. Using parted
	sudo parted  /dev/loop23 print 
